{"id":"n-20261023-o2qwle","category":"business","display_title":"Leviathan Gas Dispute Reaches London Arbitration","display_title_he":"סכסוך גז לוויתן מגיע לבוררות בלונדון","short_summary":"Dalia has filed for international arbitration in London against NewMed Energy and Ratio Energies over the cancellation of a major natural gas supply agreement from the Leviathan reservoir.","english_level":"C1","words":513,"reading_time":4,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"Globes","domain":"globes.co.il","url":"https://www.globes.co.il/news/article.aspx?did=1001560603","title":"דליה גוררת את ניו מד ורציו לבוררות בינלאומית בלונדון על עסקת הגז מלוויתן","published_at":"2026-10-23T05:39:00.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-23T05:39:00.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-23T06:22:13.803Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["A high-stakes commercial dispute concerning the largest private natural gas supply agreement in Israel's history has officially escalated into international legal proceedings. According to disclosures published by Mishk Energy, a key co-owner of Dalia Power Energies, Dalia has formally filed a request for an arbitration process at the London Court of International Arbitration against NewMed Energy, which is controlled by Yitzhak Tshuva's Delek Group, alongside Ratio Energies.","The underlying controversy stems from a massive agreement signed last May, which was specifically designed to supply natural gas to two new power generation plants that Dalia is currently developing: the Dalia 2 facility located at the Tzafit site and the Avshal plant at the Eshkol site, representing the single largest power station successfully privatized from the Israel Electric Corporation. The commercial arrangement was structured as a twenty-year commitment spanning from 2030 through 2050, carrying a baseline price of $4.7 per thermal unit, with specific provisions establishing a price renegotiation window in the year 2041 within a ten percent margin in either direction. Within the Leviathan partnership, NewMed holds approximately forty-five percent of the massive reservoir and accounts for roughly three-quarters of the transaction volume, while Ratio holds the remaining balance.","However, in late September, right before a holiday and immediately following the close of market trading, NewMed and Ratio suddenly declared the contract null and void. They asserted that specific precedent conditions concerning regulatory clearance from the Competition Authority and the finalization of financing had failed to be satisfied. Dalia immediately rejected this cancellation notice completely, maintaining that the move was legally invalid, contradictory to the contractual provisions, and therefore entirely without legal force. Approximately two weeks after the cancellation attempt, the Competition Authority officially issued its decision approving the vast majority of the contract, barring only certain restrictions regarding the selling of gas to third parties. Dalia consequently argued that this regulatory clearance completely fulfilled all necessary conditions, meaning the agreement was never actually canceled and should legally enter into force.","Industry analysts, such as Lior Wieder who owns the \"Mikhpil Revah\" information service, previously suggested that the sellers' cancellation notice might have been an aggressive negotiation tactic aimed at reopening the contract pricing terms upward. This aggressive maneuver allegedly sought to capitalize on soaring local demand for natural gas alongside potentially more lucrative export opportunities. This landmark legal battle also marks the second London arbitration currently active within the domestic natural gas market, following a separate ongoing dispute concerning gas supplies from the Tamar reservoir to the Israel Electric Corporation. Furthermore, this represents the very first business transaction where NewMed and Ratio are selling natural gas from Leviathan in a joint manner, operating entirely separately from Chevron, which serves as the primary operator of the reservoir. Within the current London arbitration framework, Dalia is specifically seeking three distinct declaratory judgments: first, that the cancellation by the sellers on September 24 was executed without authorization and contrary to law; second, that the agreement was never actually terminated because the regulatory approval was granted within the designated timeframe; and third, that the ambitious contract should officially enter into full operational force."],"facts":[{"fact":"Dalia has initiated international arbitration proceedings at the London Court of International Arbitration (LCIA) against NewMed Energy and Ratio Energies regarding a natural gas supply deal from the Leviathan reservoir.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The disputed agreement, signed in May, involves supplying gas for two new power plants, Dalia 2 and Avshal, over a 20-year period from 2030 to 2050.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"In late September, NewMed and Ratio announced the cancellation of the deal, citing the non-fulfillment of precedent conditions, but Dalia rejected the cancellation outright, arguing that regulatory approval from the Competition Authority was obtained within the required timeframe.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"high-stakes","lemma":"high-stakes","pos":"adjective","he":"עמוס סיכונים, דרמטי","name":false},{"text":"commercial","lemma":"commercial","pos":"adjective","he":"מסחרי","name":false},{"text":"dispute","lemma":"dispute","pos":"noun","he":"סכסוך, מחלוקת","name":false},{"text":"concerning","lemma":"concerning","pos":"preposition","he":"בנוגע ל-, בקשר ל-","name":false},{"text":"largest","lemma":"large","pos":"adjective","he":"הגדול ביותר","name":false},{"text":"private","lemma":"private","pos":"adjective","he":"פרטי","name":false},{"text":"natural","lemma":"natural","pos":"adjective","he":"טבעי","name":false},{"text":"gas"